The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2319 (2016) and 2332 (2016), and the relevant decisions of the OPCW Executive Council,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,
Recalling the establishment of the OPCW-UN Joint Investigative Mechanism by resolution 2235 (2015) and the extension of its mandate by resolution 2319 (2016) until 17 November 2017,
Taking note of the report of the Joint Investigative Mechanism of 26 October 2017 and expressing grave concern at its findings and at continuing reports of chemical weapons use in the Syrian Arab Republic, while stressing the need for the Mechanism’s conclusions to be based on credible, verifiable evidence and impartial and objective examination,
Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons constitutes a serious violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security, and that those responsible for any such use must be held accountable,
Stressing the need for the Joint Investigative Mechanism to continue its work in an impartial, objective and independent manner, and for all parties to cooperate fully with it, including by providing unhindered and safe access,
Reaffirming the importance of the Security Council maintaining unity and consensus on the question of chemical weapons in Syria and of avoiding politicization,
Determining that the situation in the Syrian Arab Republic continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the mandate of the OPCW-UN Joint Investigative Mechanism, as set out in resolution 2235 (2015) and extended by resolution 2319 (2016), for a technical period of 30 days, until 18 December 2017, without prejudice to a comprehensive review of its mandate and without constituting a precedent for further extension;
2. Requests the Joint Investigative Mechanism to continue to report to the Security Council, as appropriate, on the implementation of its mandate, including on its methodology and evidentiary basis, while preserving the independence and confidentiality of its work;
3. Calls upon all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic, including the Syrian Government, and all other relevant parties to cooperate fully and without preconditions with the Joint Investigative Mechanism and the OPCW, and to ensure unhindered and safe access to all relevant sites, persons and information;
4. Reaffirms that those responsible for the use of chemical weapons must be held accountable, and urges all Member States to cooperate fully with the Joint Investigative Mechanism and the OPCW in this regard;
5. Decides to keep under review the mandate and working methods of the Joint Investigative Mechanism, in light of its forthcoming report, before considering any further extension;
6. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

